12049860,2025-02-13,Credit reporting or other personal consumer reports,Credit reporting,Incorrect information on your report,Account status incorrect,"XXXX XXXXWorld Acceptance Coorporation is improperly reporting a debt that they no longer own. They are in violation Under the FCRA, creditors and lenders are required to report accurate and current information to credit reporting agencies. If a lender has sold a debt, they no longer have an interest in that debt, and they should not report it as if they still hold it.

Here are some key points related to your question : Accuracy Requirement ( Section 1681s-2 of the FCRA ) : This section stipulates that any information reported to credit bureaus must be accurate and current. If a
lender sells a debt to another party, they can not continue to report it as their own debt. The debt should be removed or updated to reflect the current holder.

Obligation to Correct Errors ( Section 1681i ) : If a consumer disputes an item on their credit report, the lender or entity reporting that debt has an obligation to investigate and correct any inaccuracies. If a debt is sold, the reporting should reflect the new creditor, not the original one.

Transfer of Debt ( Section 1681s-2 ) : When a debt is sold or transferred, the original lender should report that the debt is no longer theirs, and the new holder of the debt should be the one reporting.

If a lender continues to report a debt after it's sold or transferred to another party, they may be in violation of the FCRA, and the consumer can dispute this with the credit bureaus. If the reporting continues improperly, consumers could potentially take legal action against the lender under the FCRA. 

So, in essence, if a lender has no further interest in a debt they've sold, they should not be reporting it as theirs, and if they do, it's considered a violation of the Fair Credit Reporting Act.",,World Acceptance Corporation,IL,60438,,Web,2025-02-21,Closed with explanation,Yes
