School Misconduct : As a member of the XXXX XXXX XXXXXXXX XXXX, I completed Navient 's School Misconduct form. Despite the fact the school through extensive evidence provided to the Dept of Education which led to the Misconduct decision, Navient has refused to refund the thousands of dollars collected post-bankruptcy and denied my claim stating 1 ) The debt had been paid and 2 ) The school was a Non Profit. In the context of school misconduct, such as in the XXXX XXXX XXXX case, the Holder Rule means that if a school is found guilty of misconduct, borrowers who attended that school can raise the same claims and defenses against the loan holder that they could have raised against the original lender, Navient ( formerly Sallie Mae ). 

Repeated CFPB Complaints : I have filed multiple complaints with the CFPB regarding this issue, but Navient continues to close these complaints without providing the legally owed refunds. 

Legal Obligations : The Holder Rule, as it applies to school misconduct in the XXXXXXXX XXXX XXXXXXXX case, ensures that if a loan is sold or assigned to another party, the new holder is bound by the terms of the original loan agreement. This includes any defenses or claims that the borrower could assert against the original lender. 

